 27. Degener (see note 13) 28. Ibid. 29. Nabbali E. M. “A ‘mad’ critique of the social model of disability,”. International Journal of Diversity in Organizations, Communities and Nations. 2009;9(4):1–12. [ Google Scholar ] 30. Beresford et al. (2010, see note 5); Russo and Shulkes (see note 5); Beresfod et al. (2016, see note 5); Nabbali (see note 29), p. 4. 31. Oliver M. “The social model of disability: Thirty years on,”. Disability and Society. 2013;28(7):1024–1026. [ Google Scholar ] 32. “They don’t consider me as a person”: Mental health and human rights in Ugandan communities. 2014. Mental Disability Advocacy Center and Mental Health Uganda, Available at http://www.mdac.org/sites/mdac.info/files/mental_health_human_rights_in_ugandan_communities.pdf . 33. Sieden S. A fuller view: Buckminster Fuller’s vision of hope and abundance for all. Studio City:: Divine Arts Media,; 2011. p. 358. (ed), [ Google Scholar ] 34. Costa L. Mad studies: What it is and why you should care. 2014. Available at https://madstudies2014.wordpress.com/2014/10/15/mad-studies-what-it-is-and-why-you-should-care-2/ 35. Frank A. W. “The standpoint of storyteller,”. Qualitative Health Research. 2000;10(3):363. doi: 10.1177/104973200129118499. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 36. Cusak P., McAndrew S., Duxbury J. et al. “An integrative review exploring the physical and psychological harm inherent in using restraint in mental health inpatient settings,”. International Journal of Mental Health Nursing. 2018;27(3):1162–1176. doi: 10.1111/inm.12432. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. McKeown M., Thomson G., Scholes A. “‘Catching your tail and firefighting’: The impact of staffing levels on restraint minimization efforts,”. Journal of Psychiatric Mental Health Nursing. 2019. pp. 131–141. 26/5–6. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] 38. Maglajlic R. A. “Co-creating the ways we carry each other: Reflections on being an ally and a double agent,”. In: Russo J., Sweeney A., editors. Searching for a rose garden,