University of Chicago Press,; 1974. The Structure of Scientific Revolutions. 1970. [ Google Scholar ] 134. Middleton W.E.K. Johns Hopkins Press; 1966. A History of the Thermometer and Its Use in Meteorology. [ Google Scholar ] 135. Rehm J., Marmet S., Anderson P., Gual A., Kraus L., Nutt D.J., Room R., Samokhvalov A.V., Scafato E., Trapencieris M., Wiers R.W., Gmel G. Defining substance use disorders: do we really need more than heavy use? Alcohol. Alcohol. 2013;48(6):633–640. doi: 10.1093/alcalc/agt127. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 136. Compton W.M., Guze S.B. The neo-kraepelinian revolution in psychiatric diagnosis. Eur. Arch. Psychiatry Clin. Neurosci. 1995;245(4):196–201. doi: 10.1007/BF02191797. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 137. Deserno L., Heinz A., Schlagenhauf F. Computational approaches to schizophrenia: a perspective on negative symptoms. Schizophr. Res. 2017;186:46–54. doi: 10.1016/j.schres.2016.10.004. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 138. Barron D.S. Commentary: The ethical challenges of machine learning in psychiatry: A focus on data, diagnosis, and treatment. Psychol. Med. 2021:1–3. doi: 10.1017/S0033291721001008. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 139. Korth C., Fangerau H. Blood tests to diagnose schizophrenia: self-imposed limits in psychiatry. Lancet Psychiatry. 2020;7:911–914. doi: 10.1016/S2215-0366(20)30058-4. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 140. Martin B., Franck N., Cermolacce M., Coull J.T., Giersch A. Minimal self and timing disorders in schizophrenia: a case report. Front. Hum. Neurosci. 2018:12. doi: 10.3389/fnhum.2018.00132. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 141. Winter N.R., Cearns M., Clark S.R., Leenings R., Dannlowski U., Baune B.T., Hahn T. From multivariate methods to an AI ecosystem. Mol. Psychiatry. 2021:1–5. doi: 10.1038/s41380-021-01116-y. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 142. Zimmermann M., Morgan T.A., Stanton K. The severity of psychiatric dis