Finanzierung des Gesundheitswesens 2017. https://ris.bka.gv.at/eli/lgbl/WI/2017/29/A4/LWI40011797 . Zugegriffen: 28. Aug. 2022. 11. Birner A, Fülöp G, Mildschuh S, Neruda T, Röthlin F. Regionaler Strukturplan Gesundheit für Niederösterreich 2025. Ergebnisse der landesinternen Abstimmung. 2018. https://www.noegus.at/fileadmin/user_upload/Downloads_Publikationen/RSG_NOE_2025_Teil1.pdf . Zugegriffen: 28. Juli 2022. 12. Bundesministerium für Soziales, Gesundheit, Pflege und Konsumentenschutz (BMSGPK). Leistungskatalog BMSGPK 2022. 20220315, Wien 2022. https://www.sozialministerium.at/dam/jcr:c4a10158-5a38-4dcb-8771-9134d7b6d2f6/LEISTUNGSKATALOG%20BMSGPK%202022%2020220315.pdf . Zugegriffen: 1. Sept. 2022. 13. Gemeinsamer Bundesausschuss. Richtlinie über die Ausstattung der stationären Einrichtungen der Psychiatrie und Psychosomatik mit dem für die Behandlung erforderlichen therapeutischen Personal gemäß § 136a Absatz 2 Satz 1 SGB V – PPP-RL. https://www.g-ba.de/downloads/62-492-2656/PPP-RL_2021-09-16_iK-2022-01-01.pdf . Zugegriffen: 1. Sept. 2022 14. Remschmidt H, Mattejat F. The component model of treatment in child and adolescent psychiatry: theoretical concept and empirical results. Eur Child Adolesc Psychiatry. 2001;10(Suppl 1):26–45. doi: 10.1007/s007870170005. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Bundesrepublik Deutschland. Verordnung über Maßstäbe und Grundsätze für den Personalbedarf in der stationären Psychiatrie. Psychiatrie-Personalverordnung (Psych-PV) 5. April. 2017. https://www.buzer.de/s1.htm?g=Psychiatrie-Personalverordnung+-+Psych-PV&f=1 . Zugegriffen: 8. Sept. 2022. 16. Becker K. Bedeutung der Architektur in der und für die Kinder- und Jugendpsychiatrie. Z Kinder Jugendpsychiatr Psychother. 2019;47:5–8. doi: 10.1024/1422-4917/a000639. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Fricke OP, Halswick D, Längler A, Martin DD. Healing architecture for sick kids. Z Kinder Jugendpsychiatr Psychother. 2019;47:27–33. doi: 10.1024/1422-4917/a000635. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Wöckel L, Rung D, Bachmann S, Dietschi H, Wild D. Burg Lino – Ein innenarchitektonisches Konzept zur Verbesserung der stationären Behandlung in der Kinder- und Jugendpsychiatrie. Z Kinder Jugendpsychiatr Psychother. 2019;47:19–26. doi: 10.1024/1422-4917/a000641. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Thieme H, Förster G. Empfehlungen zum Raumbedarf kinder- und jugendpsychiatrischer Tageskliniken. Bundesarbeitsgemeinschaft leitender Mitarbeiter:innen des Pflege und Erziehungsdienstes kinder- und jugendpsychiatrischer Kliniken und Abteilungen e. V. https://www.bag-ped.de/sites/default/files/dateien/empfehlungen_zum_raumbedarf.pdf . Zugegriffen: 1. Sept. 2022. 20. Pollak E, Kapusta ND, Diehm R, Plener PL, Skala K. Transitions- und Adoleszenzpsychiatrie in Österreich: Eine Pilotuntersuchung zur Sicht von Expert(innen) Z Kinder Jugendpsychiatr Psychother. 2018;46:325–335. doi: 10.1024/1422-4917/a000559. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 21. Liwinski T, Romer G, Müller JM. Evaluation einer tagesklinischen Mutter-Kind-Behandlung für belastete Mütter psychisch kranker Kinder. Prax Kinderpsychol Kinderpsychiatr. 2015;64:254–272. doi: 10.13109/prkk.2015.64.4.254. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Oelkers-Ax R. „Freiwillig hätt’ ich’s nicht gemacht …“ – Erfahrungen mit Zwangskontexten und erzwungenen Aufenthalten in einer familienpsychiatrischen und -psychotherapeutischen Tagesklinik. Prax Kinderpsychol Kinderpsychiatr. 2019;68:253–270. doi: 10.13109/prkk.2019.68.4.253. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Psychosoziale Dienste Wien. https://psd-wien.at/einrichtung/kinder-und-jugendpsychiatrisches-ambulatorium-mit-tagesklinik-extended-soulspace . Zugegriffen: 5. Okt. 2022.',2,'2025-10-13 10:20:17','2025-10-13 10:20:17');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2197,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9581074','Bruno Schulz and his 1926 Article: "Regarding the Problem of Determining Hereditary Prognosis. The Affliction Prospects for Nephews and Nieces of Schizophrenics".','Kendler KS, Klee A.','2022','nieces nephews genealogical personalities personalities personalities eccentricity eugenic eugenic socialists nieces nephews nieces nephews sisters reckon descendants inquirer 1920s socialists promulgation genealogic architects sketch genealogical forschungsanstalt dfa braunschweig sanitorium buch wwi prisoner genealogical genealogical gifted mathematician esteemed erbforschung besonderer socialism flaming socialist eliot eminent genealogical expelled speculates genealogical stromgren conscientiousness disgust genealogical autobiography brake eugenicist hereditarily wwii eugenic directorship dfa fascinated erbprognose bestimmung erkrankungsaussichten neffen nichten schizophrenen affliction nephews nieces schizophrenics nieces nephews genealogical dfa quoting parish surrounds asylums requisitioned parish uncle aunt nieces nephews schizophrenics nieces nephews incontrovertibly countering algebraically eugenic genealogical sibships sibships sibships sibships bavaria eccentricity personalities unfeeling loner mistrustful taciturn parsimonious unfeeling mistrustful peculiarly drank frivolous angered drank frugal opinionated stingy conceited weepy tempered natured agitable childless jealous manslaughter gloomy itinerant disheveled lazy debauched superstitious stingy nieces nephews embezzlement jealous jealous tempered offence stepdaughter hostile angered licentiously natured childless schizoid nieces nephews fatefully precondition nieces nephews nieces nephews realizes settle personalities psychopaths psychopaths unsociable emotionless stubborn schizoid eccentrics hysterical epileptoid enfeebled alcoholics reiterate personalities wondered childless childless nieces nephews nieces nephews nieces nephews eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics eccentrics childless jealous tempered nieces nephews personalities personalities eccentricity abridged lifetimes affliction nieces nephews reproduces eccentricity subtler eccentricity eccentricity eccentricity nieces nephews eccentricity nieces nephews nieces nephews eccentrics eccentrics eugenic nieces nephews inconspicuous nieces nephews sophistication nieces nephews schizoidia genealogical eccentricity insane personalities schizotypal schizoid paranoid eccentricity eccentricity eccentricity eccentricity personalities eccentricity genealogical empirische erbprognose luxenburger kallmann socialist expulsion genealogical wwii stromgren eugenicist eugenic wwii ponder sbac109_suppl_supplementary_materials ksk astrid klee germanic ksk sketch erbprognose bestimmung erkrankungsaussichten neffen nichten schizophrenen kallmann memoriam nervenkrankheiten vereinigt symbiosis thums memoriam harcourt brace erbforschung besonderer eliot schioldann stromgren volter stromgren psychiatica teicher mendelism vererbung entstehung geistiger rungen vererbung neuentstehung monographien gesamtgebiet vererbung entstehung geistiger nachkommenschaft endogenen psychosen vererbung entstehung geistiger rassen gesellschaftsbiologie schizophrenics statistica risch zerbin genealogic prehistory strohmayer genealogie schizoids neale kosters kirkby himmerich din subordinated eugenic e1005524 luxenburger demographische engeren biologischen familie paralytikerehegatten kallmann klee kallmann neuropsychiatr stenstedt kritische biographie zerbin dokumentierte erwiderung ihn beschuldigungen accusations planck gottesman bertelsen hindsight gejman gershon lerer segman gejman gershon hochschule niedenhein biapsy biographien ruedin roelcke programm genetik forschungsanstalt verhaltnis politik rasse forschungsanstalt medizinhist kevles sbac109_suppl_supplementary_materials','Abstract Authored by one of Rüdin’s most productive colleagues, Bruno Schulz, the main goal of this article was to estimate risk for dementia praecox (DP) in nieces/nephews (n/n) of DP probands to address practical questions for genetic counseling. Schulz selected 76 primary probands from records of the genealogical department of the German Research Institute of Psychiatry who both had DP and had siblings—secondary probands—with children aged 20 years or greater. Those children had a morbid risk for DP of 1.4%, much lower than that found in the offspring of DP in Hoffmann’s study. However, the secondary probands had much lower rates of DP than the unselected siblings of DP cases studied by Rüdin. So rates of DP in n/n of DP probands are likely lower than expected theoretically because the secondary probands are being indirectly selected for a lowered genetic risk for DP by having successfully reproduced. Schulz diagnosed eccentric and other disordered personalities in the secondary probands and their spouses and presented evidence that other disordered personalities but especially eccentric personalities in parents were related to the risk of both eccentricity and DP in their children, the n/n of the DP probands. Eugenic issues play a minor role in this article. Although generally supportive of eugenic goals, historical sources document that Schulz was opposed to the National Socialists and actively attempted to protect Jews and his patients from involuntary sterilization. This complicates attempts to judge the morality of the work of the Rüdin school as a single unit. Keywords: Bruno Schulz, schizophrenia, genetics, nieces, nephews The practical problem that Schulz sought to address is clearly articulated in the opening comments of this detailed 37-page article: Within the general question of how great the expectation of illness for the relatives of the mentally ill actually is with regard to the psychoses, there is a particularly large, constantly increasing practical need to obtain information, which is as precise as possible, about how many nieces and nephews of dementia praecox patients become afflicted with dementia praecox. The more the facts of the heredity of mental illness become known, the more often particularly the brothers and sisters of patients with the most frequent mental illness, namely dementia praecox, turn to doctors with the question whether and to what extent they must reckon that their possible descendants will also become mentally ill. The more conclusively the answer to this question can be given, the greater will be the influence on the decision of the inquirer to conclude a planned marriage or not 1 p.1. Using current terminology, Schulz is seeking to address a problem of genetic counseling. Before marriage, siblings of individuals with dementia praecox sometimes want to know the empirical risk of severe mental illness in their offspring. In the discussion section, we provide historical background about the eugenics movement worldwide in the 1920s and especially in Germany as well as events following the coming to power of the National Socialists in Germany in 1933 and the promulgation of the “Law for the Prevention of Genetically Diseased Offspring” in 1934 which mandated involuntary sterilization for a list of seven specific disorders including schizophrenia. Schulz’s supervisor at the Genealogic Department of the German Research Institute of Psychiatry in Munich, Ernst Rüdin, who directed Schulz to undertake this study, was one of the main architects of that law. Before turning to the content of this article, we provide a biographical sketch of Schulz. Schulz—A Brief Biography Bruno Schulz (20 June, 1890–7 February, 1958), who succeeded Rüdin as the Director of the Genealogical Demographic Division of the German Research Institute of Psychiatry (Deutsche Forschungsanstalt für Psychiatrie or DFA) founded by Emil Kraepelin, from 1945 to 1958, was born in Braunschweig and studied Medicine at the University of Jena. He was the assistant at the Berlin Sanitorium Buch and the Jena clinic for nervous disorders for 4 years, and during World War I (WWI) was head physician of the reserve forces and was taken prisoner of war by the French. From 1924, he worked at the Demographic-Genealogical Division with Emil Kraepelin and Ernst Rüdin, focusing initially on experimental psychology 2 p. 121, and then on the genetic basis of psychiatric disorders 3 p. 45. During Rüdin’s absence from Munich, Schulz managed the genealogical-demographic department as temporary head. 4 He was a gifted mathematician and corresponded regularly with Wilhelm Weinberg, an obstetrician and one of the leading statistical geneticist of that era 5 p. 129. In 1936, his internationally esteemed publication Methodik der medizinischen Erbforschung unter besonderer Berücksichtigung der Psychiatrie 6 (Methodology of Medical Genetic Research Particularly with Regard to Psychiatry), implicitly questioned the sterilization policies of National Socialism. According to Weiss, Rüdin described him as “although not a flaming National Socialist”, he was “a national-minded man through and through 3 p. 164.” According to Eliot Slater, the eminent English Psychiatric Geneticist who studied at the Demographic-Genealogical Division in the 1930s, Schulz “…could not stand the Nazi ideology and never compromised with it 7 p. 18.” Although Schulz was deemed unacceptable by the SS, having refused to join the Nazi Party, he was nevertheless not expelled from his position, and Weiss speculates that this could be because he was not deemed important enough 3 pp. 167, 170. Another visitor to the Demographic-Genealogical Division was the Danish psychiatrist, Erik Strömgren. In a short biography of him, it was noted that while at the division “… he discussed genetic and epidemiological methodology … in particular, with Bruno Schulz, a man of great personal integrity, conscientiousness in research and “a deep disgust with the Nazi regime.” 8 p. 285. Karl Stern, a psychiatrist who also worked at the Demographic-Genealogical Division in the early 1930s, stated in his autobiography, that “I know of many Jews whom [Schulz] helped, he also applied a brake to [Rüdin]. … he often had to examine patients clinically for the question of sterilization, and he committed extraordinary distortions to save them from the knife 5 p. 130.” Nevertheless, Schulz was a devoted eugenicist and believed in the voluntary sterilization of the hereditarily diseased. After World War II (WWII), in 1947, he drew up a draft for a voluntary “Sterilization Law”, in which he defended eugenic principles 9 p. 207. Schulz took over directorship of the DFA in 1945 when Rüdin was forced to retire by the Allies. He was only appointed as associate professor for psychiatric genetics in 1949 and extracurricular professor at the Munich Medical faculty in 1954, since previously he had chosen to be disregarded for promotion due to political reasons 2 p. 121. His special field was statistical analysis of family/genetic data especially for psychiatric and neurologic disorders, and he was particularly fascinated by problems of methodology. During his lifetime, Schulz authored 51 publications, most of which concerned the risk of psychosis in families of affected probands and in the general population or methodologic issues to which he was considered to have made valuable contributions 2 p. 122. Many emerging genetic researchers, including Slater, were introduced by Schulz into the fundamentals and statistical methods of this new field 2 p. 122. Schulz’s Article We now outline the main methodological features of this study entitled “Zum Problem der Erbprognose-Bestimmung. Die Erkrankungsaussichten der Neffen und Nichten von Schizophrenen” (Regarding the Problem of Determining Hereditary Prognosis. The Risk [literally “Affliction Prospects”] for Nephews and Nieces of Schizophrenics.). First, however, we need to clarify its structure and terminology. Schulz’s goal was to study systematically, for the first time, the nieces/nephews (n/n) of dementia praecox (DP) probands. To do this, he had to first identify primary probands with DP. Then he identified their siblings, whom we will call secondary probands, who could have had children who would then be n/n of the primary DP probands. In addition, for secondary probands who had children, Schulz studied their spouses, who would be the co-parent of the n/n. Primary probands for this study were ascertained from the material in the genealogical department of the DFA without regard to information about their family history of illness. He utilized only two selection criteria (1) a clear case of DP who (2) had siblings with children aged ≥20 years. It is worth quoting his assessment methods in detail: We tried to gain as much information as possible about all of these selected cases, both about the proband and his siblings … as well as about the children of the [secondary] proband siblings – also about the fathers and mothers of these children who had married into these families… Therefore, the parish offices, the registry offices and other authorities and, insofar as their address could be obtained, the family members themselves were approached with the request for information. The family members who were found to be living in Munich and its surrounds were requested to come to the Research Institute … Medical case sheets from the clinics and asylums, police and court files, when this became relevant, were requisitioned. Although a large number of relatives did come to us, and almost all the authorities, especially the parish offices, complied with our request, it was nevertheless possible in relatively few cases to obtain sufficient information 1 p. 2–3. Schulz also notes that for older n/n, where they could not find adequate information, as occurred commonly, they sought records in the regional Munich asylum. He then reviewed the practical problems confronting this study, particularly that the n/n were often not motivated to appear or provide information as they were being located through an affected uncle or aunt. This differed from experiences at the institute when they tried to contact brothers or children of affected individuals, where the subjects were often more motivated to participate. Schulz then makes an important point about the goal of this study—to calculate an empirical risk of illness in n/n of DP patients: Considering the existing situation one could ask oneself whether it even seemed beneficial to undertake research about the nieces and nephews of schizophrenics in the way in which it is done here, whether it would not be more useful to calculate the ratio of the expected number of afflicted nieces and nephews of dementia praecox patients on the basis of the Mendelian ratios according to which dementia praecox is inherited… although the di-hybrid recessive inheritance of dementia praecox has been shown to be highly probable by Rüdin, it would nevertheless be stretching the point to view it as incontrovertibly verified… even if we had obtained full certainty about the inheritance of dementia praecox, empirically obtained figures would seem preferable to me compared to calculated ones, which have great comparative value, since in those that are not purely empirically obtained, we may entirely overlook a number of facts which can significantly influence the results.... Various other facts of this kind may perhaps not be known to us at this point. In purely empirical research, they are all automatically considered 1 p.4. Rüdin’s main motivation for his 1916 sibling study 10 was to determine whether the observed risk of illness was consistent with Mendelian expectations. Schulz’s does not have a similar prominent goal here and never attempts to use his results to support a particular Mendelian model. Thus, his goals meaningfully differ from those of Rüdin’s earlier study. In the above quote, Schulz is anticipating and countering, by two arguments, the following hypothetical argument “If we know the correct Mendelian model for DP, why study the empirical question of risk in n/n? We can just estimate it algebraically.” First, he suggests that the Mendelian model for DP proposed by Rüdin is far from certain and by implication insufficiently supported to use as a basis for risk prediction. Second, even if the theoretical model was better supported, a lot of factors—some of which he discusses later in the essay, especially differential fertility—could modify in important ways the actual observed risk. That is, for the practical problem of predicting risk of DP in relatives, with its consequences for genetic counseling/eugenic efforts, he is not ready to rely on theoretical models, instead arguing that the real-world study needs to be performed. Schulz then turns to noting an important advantage of his study coming after the 1916 study of siblings of DP probands by Rüdin 10 and the 1921 study of offspring of DP probands by Hoffman. 11 He used nearly identical ascertainment and diagnosis methods and some of the same probands, working at the same genealogical department. Thus, he can compare his results with those obtained by Rüdin and Hoffmann to illuminate possible methodological concerns with his investigation. Schulz found 76 primary DP probands meeting his entry criteria which came from 74 sibships. For some calculations, the members of these double-proband sibships were counted twice as required by the proband method developed by Wilhelm Weinberg 12 to avoid biases. The resulting 74 sibships of the primary DP probands contained a total of 563 siblings—all secondary probands—suggesting quite large sibships at this time in Catholic Bavaria. Schulz wanted to assess the representativeness of his secondary probands, and so compared in his table 1 , his series of siblings of DP probands to the much larger one studied by Rüdin. He notes the much higher percentage of siblings over the age of 40 years in his sample (190/557 = 34.1%) compared to Rüdin’s (482/4823 = 10.0%). This is a consequence of his ascertainment strategy, as he is selecting for older secondary probands by requiring them to have children aged 20 years or higher. Of more concern, however, he finds that, after an extensive section about the proper methods of age-correction, the morbid risk for DP is much lower in his sibling sample (our secondary probands) (2.44%) than in Rüdin’s entire cohort (5.35%). Table 1. Descriptions of Relatives with Eccentricity and Other Abnormal Personalities Group of Relatives Eccentric Other Abnormal Secondary probands (1) unfeeling, more of a loner, (2) mistrustful, peculiar, (3) nervous, agitated, strange, (4) taciturn, almost parsimonious, unfeeling, and (5) mistrustful, peculiarly rigid. (1) drank and smoked a lot, frivolous, (2) easily angered, (3) easily agitated, (4) mentally impaired, friendly, and (5) previously drank heavily Married in parents (1) agitated, emotionally cold, (2) emotionally cold and frugal, (3) serious, withdrawn, (4) self-opinionated, stingy, and (5) ambitious, conceited, very nervous. (1) agitated, (2) nervous, agitated, (3) weepy, (4) hot-tempered, but good-natured, and (5) easily agitable. Childless siblings of dementia praecox probands (1) very jealous, (2) peculiar, very religious, (3) died in jail (manslaughter), gloomy, (4) eccentric, always itinerant, and (5) disheveled, lazy, led a debauched life. (1) drinks a lot, (2) mentally impaired, superstitious, (3) friendly, however, nervous, (4) depressed, somewhat stingy, and (5) mentally weak. Nieces/nephews of dementia praecox probands (1) withdrawn, calculating, committed embezzlement, coarse, (2) very jealous for no reason, (3) very jealous, (4) cold, hot-tempered, sexual offence with his 10-year-old stepdaughter, and (5) cold, hostile. (1) nervous, easily angered, (2) nervous, (3) easily agitated, lives licentiously, (4) mentally impaired, and (5) nervous, but good-natured. Open in a new tab Schulz then discusses the likely sources of these differences. He is most concerned that childless siblings of the DP probands who had to be excluded from his sample but not from Rüdin’s, would have a considerably higher rate of psychopathology—both schizoid personality and DP—than the fertile siblings whom he can study. He summarizes his problem: We merely have to be clear that we are not here investigating to what average ratio nieces and nephews of dementia praecox patients fatefully become ill, with the precondition that all types of dementia praecox patients on average have the same number of nieces and nephews … Instead we are merely investigating how many of the existing nieces and nephews on average actually become ill 1 p. 11. That is, he realizes that he cannot achieve the theoretically ideal study—in which every DP patient has an equal number of n/n—having to settle for the real world with its necessary biases. Next, he compares his results to those obtained by Hoffmann in examining the offspring of DP probands. 11 We can avoid the details here and simply say the Schulz concludes that the children of his secondary probands have a risk slightly lower but broadly comparable to those found by Hoffmann in a larger sample. Schulz then reviews the results of his clinical evaluation of his secondary probands. Before doing this, however, we need to consider Schulz’s approach to the diagnosis of what he terms “abnormal personalities” or “psychopaths”. He writes In the absence of a uniform nomenclature, I believed that the difficulties of delineating and categorizing psychopaths could best be dealt with in the following way … I thought it was good to initially classify these abnormal individuals into such cases … which in any way showed a relationship in their nature with the clinical expression of dementia praecox, therefore the individuals who were unsociable, emotionless, unclear or stubborn (1st group), and those who did not show such a relationship to dementia praecox (2nd group)… As I … do not want to assume it to be absolutely certain that there always is a more than external relationship in the 1st group to dementia praecox … I wanted to avoid the name schizoid for this group, and therefore rather describe them as “eccentric”. Among these eccentrics I thus also want to include the abnormal individuals who display schizophrenia-like features, in contrast to the hysterical, unstable, depressive and agitated-epileptoid. I have summarized these as “other abnormal”, to which are added, besides the above-mentioned groups, also the mentally enfeebled and alcoholics… 1 pp. 22–23. This can be confusing so to reiterate—among the group of abnormal personalities, Schulz calls those with schizophrenia-like features “eccentric” and those without such features “other abnormal.” While not explicitly stated, our assumption is that Schulz attempted this distinction because he wondered if he would find stronger evidence for a genetic association with DP for relatives with an eccentric versus the “other abnormal” personality types. Schulz’s key results for his rough classification for his secondary probands are summarized in his table 2 . He classified them into 5 broad diagnostic categories (these results are without age-correction): normal (69.4%), eccentric (10.0%), “other abnormal” (18.7%), DP (0.5%), and “other psychoses” (1.9%). Later in his article, in his table 6, he compares these figures with the childless potential secondary probands that he had to exclude from this study. The parallel percentages for this group were: normal (58.0%), eccentric (17.2%), “other abnormal” (14.0%), DP (6.4%), and “other psychoses” (4.3%). So, Schulz’s concerns about an ascertainment bias were empirically confirmed. The childless siblings of primary DP probands have substantially higher rates of eccentric personality, other psychoses and especially DP than do the fertile siblings who are the only ones included in Schulz’s study. Table 2. Risk in Nieces and Nephews of Dementia Praecox Patients as a Function of the “Personality Diagnosis” in their Parents a Parents of Nieces and Nephews Nieces and Nephews DP Proband’s Siblings Spouse of DP Proband Sibling “Normal” “Eccentrics” “Other Abnormal” Dementia Praecox Total “Normal” “Normal” 221 9 20 – 250 “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” “Normal” 77 7 17 2 103 “Normal” “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” 47 4 14 – 65 “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” 18 6 13 2 39 “Normal” “Normal” 88.4% 3.6% 8.0% – – “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” “Normal” 74.4% 6.8% 16.5% 2.0% – “Normal” “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” 72.3% 6.1% 21.5% – – “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” “Other abnormal” or “Eccentrics” 46.2% 15.4% 33.3% 5.1% – Open in a new tab Table 9 from Schulz article. In this article, Schulz provides short summaries of individuals diagnosed as “eccentric” and “other abnormal” from four groups of relatives: secondary probands, married-in parents, childless siblings of DP probands and the target n/n. In table 1 , we present these brief summaries for the first 5 relatives in each group so that the reader can have a “clinical feeling” for these categories. Consistent with Schulz’s description, the eccentric relatives are often described as withdrawn, suspicious or jealous, emotionally cold, and odd. His “other abnormal” category, as he describes, is more varied and includes excess drinking, agitation, hot-tempered and nervous, traits at Schulz’s time as well as our own not generally considered to be closely related to risk for schizophrenia. Schulz then turns to an examination of the nieces and nephews of the DP probands (see his table 5). He ascertained 716 n/n but for his genetic analyses, his total count is 722 because of the two double proband sibling pairs being counted twice. These 722 n/n are distributed among offspring of 218 proband siblings so an average of 3.3 children per family. Schulz first reports the uncorrected rates of his four broad clinical categories in the n/n: normal (85.3%), eccentric (3.9%), “other abnormal” (10.2%), and DP (0.6%). His first impression is that the rates of abnormal personality among the n/n of DP probands is unlikely to be elevated above that seen in the general population. But no such data was currently available to him, so he suggested a different comparison—the rate of abnormal personalities in the n/n versus children of DP probands from his own sample. These 2 groups are relatively well matched for age. The rates of abnormal personalities and DP in the children of DP probands (his table 7) [normal (56.4%), eccentric (18.2%), “other abnormal” (20.0%), and DP (5.5%)] are indeed much higher than that observed in the n/n. So, using a comparison group from within his own study, he could show a much higher risk for DP and eccentricity in the children versus the n/n of DP probands. All four psychoses detected in the n/n are considered by Schulz to be DP with ages at onset of, respectively, 18, 20, 28, and 32. He reviews his age correction in detail, followed the abridged method of Weinberg which calculates a “lifetime-at-risk” measure. N/n under the age of 16 years are not counted at all, those between ages 16 years and 40 years are counted one-half and those above the age of 40 years are counted fully. These n/n are a young sample (238 are under age 16 years) and together he calculates them to have 290.5 lifetimes at risk. Thus, his final morbid risk for DP in the n/n of DP probands was 4/290.5 = 1.4%. Schulz compares this figure to that reported by Rüdin from his siblings series, Hoffmann from his direct study of children and his own small series of children and concludes “In every case the affliction probability found for the nieces and nephews remains significantly behind that for the children 1 p. 21.” He notes that no appropriate control population was then currently available with which to compare his findings. At this point, Schulz has met the main task of his inquiry—estimating the risk for DP in n/n of DP probands. However, he further examines his data to address three inter-related further questions. First, putting together his 2 categories of abnormal personality, does the risk for his various diagnostic categories in the n/n vary as a function of the diagnostic status of the parents? Our table 2 reproduces the relevant data from Schulz which addresses this question. Rates in the n/n of eccentricity, other abnormal personality, and DP are lowest in families where both parents are normal, intermediate when one has an abnormal personality and highest when both do. The risks of DP and potentially related personality traits in n/n of DP probands are quite dependent on the diagnostic status of their parents. Schulz’s second additional question is a subtler one. Does the impact of parental abnormal personality on risk in the n/n for DP and eccentricity differ if the affected parent is the sibling of the primary DP proband vs the “married in” parent? This question is also addressed in table 2 . Although based on quite small numbers, an abnormal personality in a secondary proband is associated with an increased risk for DP in their children but we see no such risk if it is the married-in individual with the abnormal personality. The third question Schulz asked of his data is whether a difference would be seen in risk to the n/n from secondary probands—their parents—who are considered eccentric vs having other abnormal personality features. The relevant data are reproduced in table 3 . The best powered comparison is between n/n when one or both parents are diagnosed “other abnormal” but neither as eccentric, vs when one parent is eccentric. The latter compared to the former group has substantially increased risk of both eccentricity and DP. Although based on very small numbers, the quite high risk for DP in the small number of n/n where both parents are eccentric is noteworthy. Schulz’s conclusions from these analyses is “… that in the majority of cases the genotype [predisposing to DP] is expressed in the recognizable psychological nature of a person …. [and] the relationship of ‘eccentricity’ with dementia praecox is more than external 1 p. 35.” Table 3. Risk in Nieces and Nephews of Dementia Praecox Patients as a Function of the Two “Personality Diagnosis” in Their Parents: Eccentricity vs Other Abnormal a Parents of the Nieces and Nephews Nieces and Nephews Dementia Praecox “Eccentrics” “Normal” or “Other Abnormal” Total Both parents “normal” – 9 241 250 One or both parents “other abnormal” (however, neither parent “eccentric”) 1 8 117 126 One parent “eccentric”, the other “normal” or “other abnormal” 2 8 60 70 Both parents “eccentric” 1 1 9 11 Both parents “normal” – 3.6% 96.4% – One or both parents “other abnormal” (however, neither parent “eccentric”) 0.8% 6.3% 92.8% – One parent “eccentric”, the other “normal” or “other abnormal” 2.8% 11.4% 85.7% – Both parents’ “eccentrics” 9.1% 9.1% 81.8% – Open in a new tab Table 10 from Schulz article. In his concluding section, Schulz notes how small his sample size is and the need for his results to be confirmed by bigger studies. He refers to both the possible “eugenic usage” of his data and its possible role in “practical marriage counseling.” His final summary is Based on the material examined here, it was shown that 1.4% of the probands’ nieces and nephews in turn became afflicted with dementia praecox or are predicted to become afflicted…. The probability of becoming afflicted if both parents are psychologically inconspicuous will be less than if one or both appear psychologically abnormal, especially if one or both are eccentric 1 p. 36 Discussion On its surface, this essay is a straightforward effort to answer a single practical question: the risk of DP in the nieces and nephews of DP patients. However, in addition to producing an answer to that question—the risk is around 1.4%—three other features of this article are noteworthy. First, the methodological sophistication of Schulz’s analyses is evident as best illustrated in his discussion about the impact of differential fertility. He was concerned about the possibility that, in siblings of DP probands, there would be a relationship between fertility and risk for DP and related personality disorders. He empirically tests for such a bias, which he confirms. He therefore makes it clear that he cannot answer that ideal question—the risk if “all types of dementia praecox patients on average have the same number of nieces and nephews 1 p. 11.” He can only answer the question for the data at hand, where DP patients with a high risk for DP in their siblings will, on average, have fewer n/n. To the best of our knowledge, the effect of illness-associated reduction in fertility on risk of illness in various classes of relatives was not formally modeled until 30 years later 13 with follow-up studies treating this issue being published well into the 1980s. 14–16 Second, to answer the practical question with which he starts the article, Schulz could have kept his focus entirely on DP. Instead, he adds investigations of what we could now call of the schizophrenia spectrum or schizoidia in other papers in this series. This was not an entirely novel idea as Rüdin and other workers at the Genealogical department were interested in this question. 17 Indeed, the idea that various forms of eccentricity were notable in relatives of insane patients goes well back into the 19th century. 18 Furthermore, there was wide-spread interest in this question in the psychiatric community in Germany when Schulz was working on this project. 19 Schulz operationalized 2 categories of “abnormal personalities.” One of them, termed “eccentric” was noted to have “a relationship in their nature with the clinical expression of dementia praecox 1 p. 22”. A review of the brief descriptions of relatives so diagnosed ( table 1 ) confirms that the traits considered “eccentric” by Schulz would overlap with criteria for the categories of schizotypal, schizoid, and paranoid personality disorders in DSM-III. 20 The second category, which Schulz noted “did not show such a [clinical] relationship to dementia praecox 1 p. 22” he termed “other abnormal”. Schulz found that, compared to the secondary probands who had children, the rates of eccentricity but not “other abnormal personality,” were higher in the secondary probands who had not reproduced. He then made an important further test of his 2 “personality” diagnoses—whether their presence in parents predicts the risk DP and/or for eccentricity in the children, in this case the n/n of the DP probands. Indeed, he finds such an effect for eccentricity in parents compared to other abnormal personality. He goes one further step creating an intra-study experiment comparing rates of the 2 personality disorders in the offspring versus n/n of DP probands. Rates of eccentricity are over 6 times higher in the offspring than the n/n while other personalities had a 2-fold increase. So, in addition to his main focus on the risk for DP in n/n, Schulz provides, using a novel design, clear empirical evidence for a familial link between DP and eccentricity. He made yet a further test of these eccentric personality traits—whether they were of equal predictive power for offspring risk coming from an individual with versus without elevated genetic risk for DP. The risks were not equal. The rates of DP in offspring were only elevated when the parent with eccentric traits was the sibling of a patient with DP. The genetic significance of schizophrenia spectrum traits, these results suggest, differ in those at high vs low genetic risk for schizophrenia. Third, this article reflects an early shift in methodologic focus of psychiatric family studies. Since the 1980s, family studies in psychiatric genetics have sought to quantify the degree of familial aggregation by comparing the prevalence or morbid risk of illness in relatives of case versus controls. For example, in a meta-analysis of family studies of major depression, the major conclusion was that first-degree relatives of depressed probands had an approximately 3-fold increased risk of themselves developing major depression. 21 Modern observers naturally assume that family studies in psychiatric genetics had always been focused on such questions. But this is not true. Rüdin’s major 1916 study had an entirely different focus—to uncover expected Mendelian segregation pattern. 10 , 22 As the evidence accumulated over time that simple Mendelian ratios could not be found for major psychiatric disorders, 17 , 23 the family studies at the Genealogical Division began to shift their focus to the study of “Empirische Erbprognose” or “empirical hereditary prognosis”. Schulz’s study was an early example of such a shift. It made no attempt to fit the observed results into any Mendelian pattern. Rather it had the goal of finding the empirical risk of illness in a particular class of relatives. Only 2 years after the publication of this article, Schulz’s colleague Luxenburger published the first good quality estimate of the risk of DP in the general population. 24 The estimate he obtained (0.85%) demonstrated that the degree of excess risk in the n/n of DP probands was indeed modest. The transition between these 2 foci of family studies was not abrupt. For example, Kallmann’s massive family study of schizophrenia published in 1938, 25 , 26 aided by methodological advice from Schulz, while presenting a detailed range of risk statistics, also strongly advocated for a single-locus recessive Mendelian model with incomplete penetrance and a potential polygenic background. Indeed, family studies of major psychiatric disorders well into the 1950s commented on the degree to which the results were consistent with Mendelian transmission patterns. 27 As reviewed in the introduction and first article in this series, a substantial literature has accumulated about how, if at all, the history of Rüdin’s work in psychiatric genetics ought to be told in light of his substantial and well documented involvement in and support for the racial policies of the National Socialist regime in Germany including the development of the laws for forced sterilization. 3 , 28–36 Opinions have varied widely but have included advocacy of the expulsion of his work from the scientific corpus. Much less attention in this debate has been paid to the work of other key faculty at the Genealogical Division working under Rüdin. Schulz’s case well illustrates the moral ambiguities of this problem. We were able to find three independent accounts from individuals with close personal contact with Schulz in the key period between 1933 and the start of WWII—Slater, Strömgren, and Stern—all of which document his personal opposition to Nazi ideology in general and their forced sterilization program in particular. He not only expressed those opinions but acted on them at a time when such actions might have put him at personal risk and certainly denied him more advanced academic posts. Yet, like many working in human genetics in the first half of the 20th century throughout Europe and the United States, 37 Schulz was a devoted eugenicist. Indeed, he was sufficiently committed to eugenic principles to introduce legislation in Germany for voluntary sterilization when he assumed a position of more academic prominence after WWII. We will leave it to readers to further ponder these issues as we consider how to treat the scientific contributions to the field of psychiatric genetics of Rüdin and his colleagues. Supplementary Material sbac109_suppl_Supplementary_Materials Click here for additional data file. (780.3KB, docx) Acknowledgments Translations from the German were performed by AK with the assistance of KSK. The authors report no conflicts of interest. Contributor Information Kenneth S Kendler, Department of Psychiatry, Virginia Institute of Psychiatric and Behavioral Genetics, Medical College of Virginia, Virginia Commonwealth University, Richmond, VA, USA. Astrid Klee, Department of Germanic Languages and Literatures, University of Toronto, ON, Canada. Funding Statement This supplement is sponsored by Professor Kenneth S. Kendler MD. Author Contributions The concept of this paper and the first draft was developed by KSK on the basis of the translation of this article performed by AK. The biographical sketch was prepared by AK. Both authors reviewed and revised the first draft. References 1. Schulz B. Zum Problem der Erbprognose-Bestimmung: Die Erkrankungsaussichten der Neffen und Nichten von Schizophrenen. Zeitschrift für die gesamte Neurologie und Psychiatrie. 1926;102(1):1–37. [ Google Scholar ] 2. Kallmann FJ. In Memoriam: Bruno Schulz; 1890–1958. Archiv fur Psychiatrie und Nervenkrankheiten, Vereinigt mit Zeitschrift fur die Gesamte Neurologie und Psychiatrie. 1958;197(2):121–123. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 3. Weiss SF. The Nazi Symbiosis: Human Genetics and Politics in the Third Reich. Chicago, IL: University of Chicago Press; 2010. [ Google Scholar ] 4. Thums K. In Memoriam Bruno Schulz, 1890–1958. Anthropol J. 1958;22:307–309. [ Google Scholar ] 5. Stern K. The Pillar of Fire. New York: Harcourt, Brace and Company; 1951. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Schulz B. Methodik der medizinischen Erbforschung: unter besonderer Berücksichtigung der Psychiatrie. Berlin: Thieme; 1936. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Slater E. Man, Mind, and Heredity: Selected Papers of Eliot Slater on psychiatry and genetics. Baltimore: Johns Hopkins University Press; 1971. [ Google Scholar ] 8. Schioldann JS, Stromgren L.. Erik Robert Volter Stromgren. Acta Psychiatica Scandinavica 1996;94:283–302. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 9. Teicher A. Social Mendelism: Genetics and the Politics of Race in Germany, 1900–1948. Cambridge: Cambridge University Press; 2020. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Rüdin E. Studien über Vererbung und Entstehung geistiger St ö rungen. I. Zur Vererbung und Neuentstehung der Dementia praecox (Studies on the inheritance and origin of mental illness. I. The problem of the inheritance and primary origin of dementia praecox). In: Monographien aus dem Gesamtgebiet der Neurologie und Psychiatrie, Number 12. Berlin: Springer; 1916. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Hoffmann H. Studien über Vererbung und Entstehung geistiger Störungen: II. Die Nachkommenschaft bei endogenen Psychosen. In: Studien über Vererbung und Entstehung geistiger Störungen. Berlin: Springer; 1921:4–231. [ Google Scholar ] 12. Weinberg W. Effects of selection in biological-statistical problems. Archiv fur Rassen-und Gesellschaftsbiologie. 1913;10:417–451. [ Google Scholar ] 13. Essen-Moller E. The calculation of morbid risk in parents of index cases. as applied to a family sample of schizophrenics. Acta Genetica et Statistica Medica. 1955;5:334–342. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 14. Risch N. Estimating morbidity risks in relatives: the effect of reduced fertility. Behav Genet. 1983;13(5):441–451. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 15. Kendler KS. Fitness and the risk of illness and “spectrum disorder” in offspring, parents, and siblings. Behav Genet. 1986;16(4):417–431. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 16. Kendler KS, MacLean CJ.. The impact of altered fitness on the risk of illness in relatives. Genet Epidemiol. 1989;6(4):481–491. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 17. Zerbin-Rüdin E, Kendler KS.. Ernst Rüdin (1874-1952) and his genealogic-demographic department in Munich (1917–1986): an introduction to their family studies of schizophrenia. Am J Med Genet. 1996;67(4):332–337. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 18. Kendler KS. The prehistory of psychiatric genetics: 1780-1910. Am J Psychiatry. 2021;178:490–508. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 19. Strohmayer W. Zur Genealogie der Schizophrenie und des Schizoids. Zeitschrift für die gesamte Neurologie und Psychiatrie. 1925;95(1):194–217. [ Google Scholar ] 20. American Psychiatric Association. Diagnostic and Statistical Manual of Mental Disorders. 3rd ed. Washington, DC: American Psychiatric Association; 1980. [ Google Scholar ] 21. Sullivan PF, Neale MC, Kendler KS.. Genetic epidemiology of major depression: review and meta-analysis. Am J Psychiatry. 2000;157(10):1552–1562. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 22. Kendler KS. Rüdin’s 1916 monograph: on the inheritance and primary origin of dementia praecox. Schizophr Bull. 2022;48(S1):S8–S19. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 23. Kosters G, Steinberg H, Kirkby KC, Himmerich H.. Ernst R ü din’s unpublished 1922–1925 study “inheritance of manic-depressive insanity”: genetic research findings subordinated to eugenic ideology. PLoS Genet. 2015;11(11):e1005524. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 24. Luxenburger H. Demographische und psychiatrische Untersuchungen in der engeren biologischen Familie von Paralytikerehegatten. Zeitschrift für die gesamte Neurologie und Psychiatrie. 1928;112(1):331–491. [ Google Scholar ] 25. Kallmann FJ. The Genetics of Schizophrenia. New York: J.S. Augustin; 1938. [ Google Scholar ] 26. Kendler KS, Klee A.. The place of Franz Kallmann’s 1938 “the genetics of schizophrenia” in the history of psychiatric genetics. Am J Med Genet Part B Neuropsychiatr Genet. 2022;189:26–36. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 27. Stenstedt AA. A study in manic-depressive psychosis; clinical, social and genetic investigations. Acta psychiatr Neurol Scand. 1952;28(79):1–111. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 28. Weber MM. Ernst Rüdin: Eine kritische Biographie. Berlin: Springer-Verlag; 1993. [ Google Scholar ] 29. Weber MM. Ernst Rüdin, 1874-1952: a German psychiatrist and geneticist. Am J Med Genet. 1996;67(4):323–331. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 30. Zerbin-Rüdin E. Eine dokumentierte Erwiderung auf die gegen ihn erhobenen Beschuldigungen. (Ernst Rüdin. A Documented Reply to The Accusations Raised Against Him). Berlin: Preserved in the Archives of the Max-Planck Gesellschaft; 1986. [ Google Scholar ] 31. Gottesman II, Bertelsen A.. Legacy of German psychiatric genetics: hindsight is always 20/20. Am J Med Genet. 1996;67(4):317–322. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 32. Kendler KS. Reply to Gejman, Gershon, and Lerer and Segman. Am J Med Genet. 1997;74(4):461–463. [ Google Scholar ] 33. Gejman PV. Ernst Rüdin and Nazi euthanasia: another stain on his career. Am J Med Genet. 1997;74(4):455–456; author reply 461. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 34. Gershon ES, Rüdin E.. A Nazi psychiatrist and geneticist. Am J Med Genet. 1997;74(4):457–458; author reply 461–453. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 35. Rüdin E. Hochschule Niedenhein. University of Applied Sciences; 2016. https://biapsy.de/index.php/en/9-biographien-a-z/238-ruedin-ernst-e . Date accessed 04 May 2020. [ Google Scholar ] 36. Roelcke V. Programm und Praxis der psychiatrischen Genetik an der “Deutschen Forschungsanstalt für Psychiatrie” unter Ernst Rüdin: Zum Verhältnis von Wissenschaft, Politik und Rasse-Begriff vor und nach 1933/Programme and Practice of Psychiatric Genetics at the “Deutsche Forschungsanstalt für Psychiatrie” under Ernst Rüdin: On the Relationship between Science, Politics and the Notion of Race before and after 1933. Medizinhist J. 2002;37:21–55. [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 37. Kevles DJ. In the Name of Eugenics: Genetics and the Uses of Human Heredity. Berkeley, Los Angeles: University of California Press; 1985. [ Google Scholar ] Associated Data This section collects any data citations, data availability statements, or supplementary materials included in this article. Supplementary Materials sbac109_suppl_Supplementary_Materials Click here for additional data file. (780.3KB, docx)',2,'2025-10-13 10:20:27','2025-10-13 10:20:27');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2198,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9707497','Psychiatry''s need for Vergangenheitsbewaltigung: ''culture wars'', cognitive dissonance and coming to terms with the past.','Lepping P, Poole R.','2022','vergangenheitsbewаltigung shameful homosexuality dissonance offence defensiveness shaming constructively dissonance shameful wendy apology abuses belated sterilisation murder galton saxons apology honoured galton biometric imperialism galton bethlem galton ardent admirer galton galton galton bequeathed proponent untreatable negro aborigines orang utans freudenthal misrepresented tiedemann beneath tiedemann negro eugenic assertions contemporaneous nihilism tainted excesses narcosis contemporaneously scramble annexation subjugation eugenic emblem supremacy seep defensively imperialism supremacism culpability doctrines unsound defensiveness liberalism construing supposedly civilised injunctions oppressive domination insightless nationalist victorious furious condemnation journalist olusoga wrongly subjugated empires misappropriation provokes backlash complacent patriotism cry dissent patriotic dissent negating ahistorical amritsar jallianwala bagh massacre brigadier shoot unarmed peaceful civilians apologists instinctive excuse wrongdoing apologists perpetrators dissenting contemporaneous negate monolithic journalist sathnam sanghera inspires exceptionalism disastrous amnesia wilfully supremacist genocidal eugenic ideologically pamphlet celebrating failings unwillingness failings dissonance vergangenheitsbewaltigung vergangenheitsbewaltigung dissonance dissonance remorse wrongdoings truths pasts sequestrated cioran vergangenheitsbewaltigung sathnam sanghera homophobia shameful shameful failings lepping pictured betsi cadwaladr wrexham bangor mysore bangor finalisation murdered pimlico galton typecast museumofthemind galtons typecast labours galton willan freudenthal chequered bjpsych tiedemann negro orang outang sanghera empireland imperialism penguin ariyo brilliant davidfmarkscom bankrupt fraudulent yougov yougov mohdin olusoga theguardian olusoga historian britishness tharoor inglorious cioran heights despair','Summary UK psychiatry''s sense of self rests on being part of a socially progressive national tradition. This makes it difficult to engage with more critical narratives. The process of analysing and accepting psychiatry''s past can help our profession to get closer to its real self and on a path to a better future. Keywords: Vergangenheitsbewӓltigung, psychiatry''s past, history of Psychiatry, coming to terms, society In recent years, UK psychiatry has made tentative steps to acknowledge some of the shameful episodes from its past, from aversion therapy for homosexuality to institutional racism. Frank discussion of these aspects of our profession''s history causes a degree of cognitive dissonance or even offence to some colleagues, and we fully acknowledge this. It is hard to avoid defensiveness in response to shaming facts, even when they are perceived to belong in the remote past. This is one of the reasons psychiatry has difficulty in responding constructively to its critics. Here, we outline why we believe that it is important to have this discussion, despite mixed responses within the profession. We explore some ways to approach our collective cognitive dissonance in order to learn from our past and to truly come to terms with it, individually, as a profession, and as a society. We single out psychiatry because we are psychiatrists. Other medical specialties and scientific disciplines face their own struggles to acknowledge the impact of shameful past episodes on the present. Acknowledging the past Organised psychiatry in the UK has started to face up to its past, both distant and recent. The Royal College of Psychiatrists has taken steps to acknowledge racism in the clinical practice of psychiatry, to challenge the imperial legacy in the psychiatric curriculum, and to address the discrimination experienced by Black and minority ethnic psychiatrists. Similarly, in 2017, the then President of the Royal College of Psychiatrists, Dr Wendy Burn, issued an unequivocal apology for psychiatrists’ involvement in the use of aversion techniques to ‘cure’ gay sexual orientation. These are welcome developments, but they are just the start. Meaningful change necessitates a thorough and continuing process to eliminate the continuing effects of past abuses. We need to fully acknowledge them, including the effects on those abused. This demands tangible action in the present. German psychiatry has made a belated but concerted effort to come to terms with the profession''s involvement in the crimes of the Nazi regime. 1 The UK was a victor in the Second World War, and British psychiatry has long avoided examination of its direct involvement in the development of eugenics that was later used to justify the mistreatment, sterilisation or murder of large numbers of people with learning disabilities and chronic mental illness across Europe and North America. Psychiatry, empire, eugenics and hereditary degeneration The term ‘eugenics’ was coined by Sir Francis Galton, and the Eugenics Laboratory he founded at University College London was the key institution in the world for the development of concepts of genetic (or racial) hygiene. He classified the ‘comparative worth of different races’ into grades from A to I, arguing that Africans could never attain the average grade of Anglo-Saxons. 2 In 2021, after a lengthy investigation, University College London formally acknowledged this and made an apology, de-naming spaces on its campus that previously honoured Galton and another main advocate of eugenics, Karl Pearson (who devised many of the biometric and statistical techniques still in use today, and who propagated the idea of ‘social imperialism’). British psychiatry and psychology had close involvement in the eugenics project. Galton, for instance, worked in close collaboration with psychologists and psychiatrists at Royal Bethlem Hospital, including efforts to use photographs of patients to delineate physical signs of hereditary degeneration. 3 Galton''s ardent admirer Karl Pearson noted, without criticism, how Galton''s views on genetics and eugenics shaped his views of patients and society. Pearson wrote in 1930 in a book about Galton: ‘And we ask why, with a common environment, does one man achieve and another fail to do so? The answer can only be: Such is the law of inheritance, and that was Galton''s answer’. 4 Henry Maudsley, who bequeathed the hospital named after him, was the key UK proponent of the concept that mental illness was an untreatable genetic disorder leading to ‘hereditary degeneration’ across generations. 5 In addition, he used anatomical studies to claim that ‘the brain of the Negro […] does not reach the level of the white man''s brain’. 6 He continued to point out that ‘even’ European females had heavier brains [than Black Africans and Aborigines] and proceeded to find alleged similarities between the brains of Africans and orang-utans. 7 In 2022, Hilton and Freudenthal argued that Maudsley deliberately misrepresented Friedrich Tiedemann, 7 an anatomy professor from Heidelberg, Germany, whose research had suggested in 1837 that ‘neither anatomy nor physiology can justify our placing of [Black people] beneath the Europeans morally or intellectually’. 8 Maudsley called Tiedemann the ‘Negro''s advocate’ in this context. 6 , 7 Although modern psychiatry ought not to consider comparisons of anatomy or physiology to be relevant to the human value or abilities of different peoples, it shows that even at the time when they were considered relevant, there were differing views, and Maudsley was unequivocally in the camp of those who used the eugenic ideas of the time to justify assertions of White superiority. In the 19th century, ideas of hereditary degeneration and eugenics spread quickly in the wider population. They were intimately linked to contemporaneous ideas about racial hygiene, and they resulted in the therapeutic nihilism that tainted mental hospitals for decades, encouraging excesses such as insulin coma and deep narcosis treatments. Eugenics developed contemporaneously with the ‘scramble for Africa’, which was the most rapid period of British imperial annexation of territory and subjugation of foreign populations. The eugenic ideology of racial superiority was used to justify imperial expansion, 9 with explicit comparisons made between inferior peoples and hereditary degeneration among the lower classes, who were disproportionately represented in mental hospital populations. In our opinion, the legacy of colonial racism is seen in the modern-day disproportionate use of coercion in mental health services interactions with patients of African heritage. 10 British psychology was equally heavily engaged in the eugenics project. Modern critics such as David Marks argue that ‘from the perspective of the colonial British Empire, the eugenics mission was an emblem of ‘white supremacy’, the traces of which seep into the British Psychological Society to the present day’. 11 Marks states that institutional racism and micro-aggression against non-White psychologists are still active in the British Psychological Society, citing several recent controversies, and that racial stereotypes from the colonial past have a residual impact on psychologists’ practice and research. As psychiatrists, we wish to focus on our own profession, but Marks''s article underlines that similar imperial themes affect other scientific disciplines. Psychiatrists often react defensively to the suggestion that mental health services are structurally racist, even though this does not imply that psychiatrists are necessarily individually racist. However, in our opinion, there is an obvious link between imperialism, White supremacism, concepts of eugenics, hereditary degeneration and modern structural racism. Even psychiatrists who do not agree that psychiatry bears some culpability for eugenics must acknowledge that late Victorian psychiatrists could not have remained unaffected by the pervasive doctrines of British racial superiority of their time, and that modern psychiatry was built on these unsound foundations. This defensiveness may extend to psychiatrists from non-White backgrounds. The reasons for this are complex, and they cannot be appropriately explored by two male White psychiatrists in a brief article. Neo-liberalism and culture wars In the last few decades, we have increasingly seen movements across the world that challenge dominant historical narratives. This is particularly uncomfortable for the UK because of our own colonial and imperial past. British politicians and media continue to project a positive image of the British Empire, construing it as the time when Britain was ‘Great’. The British supposedly civilised the world through propagation of their civil institutions and legal system. Injunctions to be proud of the Empire are commonly accompanied by denial of the oppressive and destructive consequences of domination of one country by another. Surveys suggest that only the Dutch surpass the British in their insightless pride in their imperial past. 12 The Second World War and the Empire arguably form the strongest elements in the nationalist narrative of White Britain, reinforced in the education system, political rhetoric, the media and entertainment. Voices that are critical of this benign and victorious historical narrative provoke furious condemnation from authority and the media. 13 The British-Nigerian journalist David Olusoga puts it thus: ‘If you have been told a version of your history and that is part of your identity, it''s very difficult when people like me come along and say: ‘There are these chapters [that you need to know about].’ People feel – wrongly in my view – that their history is being undermined by my history. But my history isn''t a threat to your history. My history is part of your history’. 13 From the point of view of the peoples that have been subjugated, all empires are built on racism, oppression, misappropriation and exploitation. Despite strong support from the historical record, pointing this out provokes defensive counter-attack and a refusal to engage with alternative views. This is seen in negative reactions to the Black Lives Matter movement. Such diverging voices therefore have difficulty in finding a platform in popular media. This prevailing narrative has not only had a profound influence on recent political decisions, but it has also provoked a backlash by British people from ethnic minorities and many young people in general, who feel that their values are not represented by this complacent conservative stance. The binding of British patriotism to pride in the Empire has become a battle cry for those who wage so-called ‘culture wars’, where voices that dissent from the narrative accepted by the political right are defined as non-patriotic and thus anti-British. Interestingly, dissent from the prevailing narrative has always been acknowledged, negating the idea that values were fundamentally different in the 19th and early 20th centuries, and that any judgement of past actions is therefore ahistorical. A good example of this is the aftermath of the Amritsar (Jallianwala Bagh) massacre, which took place on the 13 April 1919. At the time, many people in Britain were highly critical of Brigadier General Dyer''s decision to shoot hundreds of unarmed and peaceful civilians in cold blood. However, apologists and supporters of Dyer immediately started a successful public rehabilitation campaign, 14 the tone of which was remarkably similar to today''s so-called ‘culture wars’. We fully acknowledge that historical events can be seen in different ways, some that focus on events and some that use a lens of values, either past or present. Criticism of the actions of Empire or past psychiatrists are not new or in any way restricted to modern interpretations of human rights. The instinctive intellectual response is to excuse past wrongdoing on the basis that values were different then. In reality, there was a range of opinion and values at the time, and our perceptions of the values of the past have been strongly influenced by those who control historical accounts, among them many apologists of Empire. Real harms occurred, even if perpetrators were insensitive to them at the time. The many dissenting contemporaneous voices negate the idea that people in the past adhered to a single monolithic ethic that supported the dominant view. The past is alive in the present In sharp contrast to present political rhetoric in Britain, the reputation of the British Empire in its former colonies is nothing like as charitable. 14 Furthermore, the consequences of Empire for British society today are significant. As the British journalist and author Sathnam Sanghera puts it: ‘The manner in which our imperial history inspires a sense of exceptionalism results in dysfunctional politics and disastrous decision-making. Our collective amnesia about the fact that we were, as a nation, wilfully white supremacist and occasionally genocidal, and our failure to understand how this informs modern-day racism, are catastrophic’. 9 It is because the imperial narrative still matters today that psychiatry ought to take note. Victorian attitudes towards race were shaped by a eugenic science that was flawed and ideologically distorted, to which psychiatry significantly contributed. Being part of the narrative makes it more difficult for psychiatrists to fully acknowledge its consequences in the here and now. In keeping with this, UK psychiatry has invested much of its pride and sense of self in being part of a national tradition that considers itself to be socially progressive. The Royal College of Psychiatrists’ recent pamphlet Celebrating Our History demonstrates this, focusing on the organisation''s progress towards inclusivity while attributing deviations to the failings of individuals. An unwillingness to recognise systematic failings makes it more difficult to engage readily with other narratives or to acknowledge challenging facts. A deeper analysis creates cognitive dissonance over who we are and over our role within the life of the nation. This is hard to bear, and it is more comfortable to simply reject any re-evaluation of the foundations of our professional pride. Vergangenheitsbewältigung ‘Coming to terms with one''s past’ may be as good a translation as we can make for the German word Vergangenheitsbewältigung . It describes the process of analysing and accepting one''s past with all its failures, flaws and moral ambiguities. The aim is to get closer to one''s real self, learn from past mistakes and develop concepts for a better future. The concept is valuable for individuals and professions as well as for countries or nations. Germany went through this process after the Second World War. Many of these stories are full of personal guilt and responsibility, moral injury, shame and suffering. They are about collective failure as well as collectively building a better future. The initial dilemma is the tension between the self-esteem we get from being part of a benign national and professional narrative and the cognitive dissonance that will be caused and has to be worked through when we challenge this narrative. While we fully recognise and personally experience this dissonance, if we are serious in our remorse for past wrongdoings, we must find a way to engage with historical truths that allows us to come to terms with our own, our country''s and our profession''s pasts, while preserving self-esteem. In a psychotherapeutic sense, shame needs to be integrated and not sequestrated. The philosopher Cioran believed that failures are much more important than successes because, when analysed, they get much closer to one''s actual true self, allowing a person or a nation to come to terms with the past and learn from it for the future. 15 This can be painful, which makes it important to see the process itself as something positive, leading to a more authentic professional self-awareness. A Vergangenheitsbewältigung process of coming to terms with the past might become a source of a new sense of self, integrity and pride. An honest analysis that acknowledges past failures can build a better future. There are a number of possible ways in which this can happen. Sathnam Sanghera and others argue for a broadening of the national curricula in schools and universities 9 to widen our understanding of our own past with regard to Empire. This should not only include the suffering in the colonies at the hand of the British and their colonial allies but also the negative consequences of Empire in the UK, given that the wealth the Empire created was only shared among a few, usually already wealthy, people. Psychiatry may equally need to widen its curricula to acknowledge the impact up to today of its involvement in eugenics, homophobia and dangerous, ineffective treatments such as insulin coma therapy. Telling the complete story can be part of a process that helps us come to terms with the shameful aspects of our past without losing self-esteem. Accepting our complex past in its entirety and learning from it can be a source of pride. Psychiatry has special understanding of self-esteem, shame, stigma and the development of the self. Furthermore, whether we like it or not, psychiatrists exercise authority on behalf of the state. If we can examine and come to terms with shameful aspects of our past and present, we may have something to say to the nation about how to deal with past failings. This includes a willingness to learn about and acknowledge what has happened and to act on that knowledge to make things different in the future. Just like coming to terms with personal histories, analysing professional and national failures, and not just successes, can show a route to a more mature society. It can help heal the power difference in doctor–patient relationships and lead to an improvement in the relationship between psychiatrists and those who use our services. A critical part of this process is to find space to examine this as individuals and as a profession, in order to come to terms with our failures and move forward. Biographies Professor Peter Lepping (pictured) works as a Consultant Liaison Psychiatrist for Betsi Cadwaladr University Health Board in Wrexham, and at Bangor University and Mysore Medical College and Research Institute. Rob Poole is Professor of Social Psychiatry at Bangor University; he received the Royal College of Psychiatrists Lifetime Achievement Award in 2017. Author contributions P.L. wrote the first draft. Both authors worked on the concept, revision and finalisation of the manuscript. Funding None. Declaration of interest None. References 1. Müller T. Remembering psychiatric patients murdered by the Nazi regime in Germany. Lancet 2018; 5 (10): 789–90. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 2. Davies N. Europe: A History. Pimlico, 1996. [ Google Scholar ] 3. Galton''s Asylum Photos: Typecast at UCL ( https://museumofthemind.org.uk/blog/galtons-asylum-photos-typecast-at-ucl [cited 21 Aug 22]). 4. Characterization Pearson K., especially by letters. In The Life, Letters and Labours of Francis Galton : 443. University of London. 5. Godfrey B, Lawrence P. Crime and Justice 1750–1950: 113. Willan Publishing, 2005. [ Google Scholar ] 6. Maudsley H. The Physiology and Pathology of the Mind. Appleton, 1867. [ Google Scholar ] 7. Hilton C, Freudenthal R. Learning from the past: inequalities and discrimination in psychiatry''s chequered history. BJPsych Bull 2022; 46: 224–8. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 8. Tiedemann F. On the brain of the Negro, compared with that of the European and the orang-outang [translated from German]. Philos Trans R S London 1836; 126: 497–527. [ Google Scholar ] 9. Sanghera S. Empireland: How Imperialism Has Shaped Modern Britain. Penguin Books, 2021. [ Google Scholar ] 10. Barnett P, Mackay E, Matthews H, Gate R, Greenwood H, Ariyo K, et al. Ethnic variations in compulsory detention under the Mental Health Act: a systematic review and meta-analysis of international data. Lancet Psychiatry 2019; 6: 305–17. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] 11. Marks DF. ‘Brilliant’ – but Censored ( www.davidfmarkscom.wordpress.com/2022/01/16/the-british-psychological-society-as-a-morally-bankrupt-and-fraudulent-enterprise/ [cited 21 Aug 2022]). 12. Smith M. How Unique Are British Attitudes to Empire? YouGov, 2021 ( www.yougov.co.uk/topics/international/articles-reports/2020/03/11/how-unique-are-british-attitudes-empire [cited 21 Aug 2022]). 13. Mohdin A. David Olusoga on race and reality. Guardian , 7 Jun 2021. ( https://www.theguardian.com/culture/2021/jun/07/david-olusoga-race-reality-historian-black-britishness [cited 21 Aug 2022]). 14. Tharoor S. Inglorious Empire: What the British Did to India: 173. Hurst and Company London, 2017. [ Google Scholar ] 15. Cioran E. On the Heights of Despair. University of Chicago Press, 1996. [ Google Scholar ]',2,'2025-10-13 10:20:41','2025-10-13 10:20:41');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2199,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC1188522','McGill University, Department of Psychiatry 50th anniversary.','Pinard G, Young SN.','1993','schally','Full text PDF Previous 141 142 Next Selected References These references are in PubMed. This may not be the complete list of references from this article. Schally A. V. Some notes on the background of the isolation, determination of structure, synthesis, and early clinical trials of the luteinizing hormone- and follicle-stimulating hormone-releasing hormone. Am J Obstet Gynecol. 1976 Aug 15;125(8):1142–1147. doi: 10.1016/0002-9378(76)90822-x. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',3,'2025-10-13 10:20:55','2025-10-13 10:20:55');
INSERT INTO corpus VALUES(2200,'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7137658','What should we do to promote a social perspective in psychiatry?','Becker T, Rüsch N.','2016','priebe brilliant conundrums decisively priebe priebe amazing rightly priebe priebe modernisation modernisation rightly fertilise admittedly wwii conceptualisations marxian impinge onslaught modernisation priebe capitalist petrol grapple aptly priebe subsystem dwindle thrust priebe okologie gehirns systemische sichtweise priebe s2045796016000147 hariri vaop ncurrent mp201682a','The special paper by Priebe ( 2016 ) is a stimulating and brilliant narrative review of some of the evidence on the social determinants of mental health, on some of the conceptual conundrums of the field and on the importance of the social context in shaping mental ill health, mental disorders and psychiatric practice. The paper reviews fields of work that may help advance our knowledge on mental suffering, psychiatric disorders and mental health care using a social perspective or paradigm, and it covers sub-headings such as the historical aspects of the social paradigm (late 18th and 19th century history having decisively shaped the history of psychiatry both as a science and a field of mental health care practice), the theme of social determinants of mental ill health and what could be called the ‘political mission’ of psychiatry. The special paper focuses on social integration of people with mental illness, the issue of social isolation, social networks and social interventions, which are upheld by very strong evidence (regarding their weight in causing, shaping, helping overcome and treating mental disorders). Social context and social interactions and the social construct aspect of diagnostic concepts in psychiatry are all addressed, and in the reflection of the ‘way forward’ for the field collaboration with partners (beyond sociology and psychology) such as geography, economics, philosophy, linguistics and the arts is considered to provide a promising path for social psychiatry research as a project in advancing our knowledge on how to understand and alleviate mental suffering. Commenting on Priebe''s paper we briefly outline a modified or additional perspective. There is ample reason for a ‘technical’ professional discourse on specific illness-related themes such as psychiatric nosology and diagnosis, psychiatric therapeutics ranging from individual interventions all the way to milieu aspects in treatment and the overall configuration of the mental health care system. Different from the approach developed by Priebe, we would argue that, along with an overwhelming process of modernization in post-World War II European societies there has been an enormous growth in our knowledge on mental illness pathogenesis and the wealth of the therapeutic armamentarium. This growth of knowledge and evidence applies to pharmacological interventions (with a lot of the work not being ground-breaking but implying steady growth in differentiated evidence), a more substantial knowledge of the potential and limitations of treatment algorithms and an amazing growth of research on psychological interventions. Research into cognitive behavioural therapy, cognitive remediation, meta-cognitive training, solution-focused psychotherapy and social skills training has been helpful and, as rightly pointed out by Priebe, we have seen great advances in what we know about the ‘grand’ factors of effectiveness in psychotherapeutic interventions. Also, there has been high-quality work on evidence-based psychotherapy interventions for people with psychotic disorders. We do agree with Priebe that there is no solid evidence of a tidal change in the prognosis and long-term outcome of people with severe mental illness going along with this historic modernisation of the mental health care field but we would argue that these various threads of modernisation have altogether changed mental health care in high-resource countries (i.e., where and to whom it is available). As the paper rightly points out, care systems have improved in terms of resource input, and staff is currently more highly qualified than they were in the 1960s. However, we think the change in therapeutic approaches, with a host of new technical interventions and a more community-oriented set-up of the care system does reflect major change in the way mental health problems are being addressed. Adopting an optimistic view, we could speculate that approaches focusing on the biological understanding of mental illness on the one hand and on social interventions on the other hand are not only at odds or conflicting (e.g., due to competition for scarce research funding and mutually exclusive paradigms), but may also complement and cross-fertilise each other. Admittedly, there is little empirical support for this optimism, but anecdotally some proponents of biological psychiatry in the post WWII era were also engaged in social psychiatric reforms and recent conceptualisations try to integrate neurobiological and psychosocial aspects of mental illness (Fuchs, 2005 ). We would further argue that today''s research efforts on gene-environment interactions (or, more broadly, on interactions of individual disposition and environmental agents) substantiate a position reminiscent of early Marxian philosophy, e.g., that epigenetic mechanisms link socioeconomic status to depression-related brain function in high-risk adolescents (Swartz et al . 2016 ), in other words that the brain is socially shaped and that social forces impinge directly on ‘nature’ (the brain being part of ‘nature’). We can thus state that poverty, social inequality, social class and the onslaught of socioeconomic adversity shape the very human brain, its function and the way genes are expressed. Thus, the brain itself is ‘social’, and social adversity produces a ‘footprint’ in the human body/brain. Thus, the social is everywhere, it is in the treatment ‘algorithms’, in the systems and routines of ‘evidence-based medicine’, it is in rehabilitation and in amygdala function (Swartz et al . 2016 ). It is true that we lack the evidence that this hypothesised modernisation process has changed the long-term course of psychotic disorders. However, we can have the impression that the potential for socially integrated living outside institutions has improved for people with severe mental illness and care systems have moved in the direction of community arrangements. In agreement with Priebe, we could say that all the psychiatric ‘technical’ innovation has been part of societal change favouring social integration (and inclusion) of people sharing various types of minority status. These changes, in countries of Western Europe such as Italy, have spanned diverse groups and societal conflicts such as the unemployed and trade union organisations, women''s rights issues, gay and lesbian minority group issues and the situation of people with mental illness with a tidal wave of societal reform movements beginning in the 1960s and gaining momentum throughout the 1970s in Capitalist welfare state societies that were experiencing long-term economic growth (and the first petrol crisis in 1973). Thus, the reform movement in the mental health care field was contingent upon wider societal change but with no natural or ‘set’ link between the agenda of societal reform and mental health care improvement. We believe one way towards moving forward psychiatric research today is to engage in high-quality research on poverty in rich Western societies, and on the role of societal inequality and migration. We would argue that the questions of how to advance the modern ‘social state’ and of how to formulate welfare policies in the 21st century are pertinent to the field of social psychiatry research (as to many other fields). Of course, these issues are important not only for those with mental health problems and severe mental disorders but they are important to many subgroups in society who grapple with the current set-up. Very aptly, the paper by Priebe points to the wide range of humanities, social sciences and linguistics that may contribute to advancing the field of social psychiatry research. But we would suggest that the field should be defined more broadly than by just looking at the situation of people with mental illness, i.e., the societal/cultural/scientific subsystem of psychiatry and mental health services. The question is raised of how mental suffering will be framed in coming decades, to what extent societal solidarity with those suffering from schizophrenia will persist or grow (or dwindle), whether and how neo-liberal thinking will link with the paradigms of empowerment and recovery, and what fields of work mental health professionals and researchers will concentrate on. Also, the question will be whether wider societal reform movements will join forces with the mental health care sector or take interest in psychiatric issues and to what extent mental health issues will shape the discourse on and our coping with international mass migration that is currently shaping the face of Europe. Using the thrust of the paper by Priebe, we should turn to social science and wider societal movements in our attempt to understand where psychiatry and mental health care will be moving and where social psychiatric research should be heading to better understand mental health issues in the world around us that is undergoing rapid change. References Fuchs T (2005). Ökologie des Gehirns – Eine systemische Sichtweise für Psychiatrie und Psychotherapie [Ecology of the brain. A systemic view for psychiatry and psychotherapy]. Nervenarzt 76, 1–10. [ DOI ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Priebe S (2016). A social paradigm in psychiatry – themes and perspectives. Epidemiology and Psychiatric Sciences, in press. doi: 10.1017/S2045796016000147. [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ] Swartz JR, Hariri AR, Williamson DE (2016). An epigenetic mechanism links socioeconomic status to changes in depression-related brain function in high-risk adolescents. Molecular Psychiatry. doi: 10.1038/mp.2016.82 Retrieved 8 June 2016 from http://www.nature.com/mp/journal/vaop/ncurrent/full/mp201682a.html . [ DOI ] [ PMC free article ] [ PubMed ] [ Google Scholar ]',5,'2025-10-13 10:21:06','2025-10-13 10:21:06');
